+*   **Limitations:** No interactive frame rates currently [S1][S2, unofficial]. Limited arithmetic (no encrypted-encrypted multiplication or comparison) [S1][S2, unofficial]. Simplified transfer functions (cannot support complex user-defined TFs easily due to lack of comparison and conditional logic) [S1][S2, unofficial]. Network bandwidth requires significant transfer if large encrypted data amounts are moved, though server-only model minimizes this compared to trusted-client models [S1]. Cache efficiency: Naive 3D BigInteger array storage leads to poor cache usage; Morton order is suggested for improvement [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Based on the Paillier cryptosystem developed by Pascal Paillier in 1999 [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Development:** The method was proposed by Sebastian Mazza, Daniel Patel, and Ivan Viola in 2020 [S1][S2, unofficial]. It addresses the need for privacy-preserving volume rendering in domains like medical imaging (CT, MRI, PET) and seismic exploration [S1].
-*   **Context:** Prior approaches included Mohanty et al. [22] (two-server model, fixed TF, potential privacy leaks) and Chou and Yang [3] (obfuscation-based, not cryptographically secure) [S1][S2, unofficial]. This method aims to be "secure by design" using cryptographic hardness [S1][S2, unofficial].
